WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, Episode 114.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.180
It's June 27th, 2026.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.160
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.300
Mars is gliding past the Plyese Star Cluster in the early morning sky,

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.900
offering a rare, close-pairing visible before sunrise.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:22.540
Mars currently sits in tourists and appears in the pre-dawn sky.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.700
Today it lies less than five degrees from the famous Plyese

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.340
Star Cluster designated M45.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:35.520
Observers who rise early can spot the pairing low in the east before sunrise washes it out.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:41.460
The geometry places Mars on a line of sight that brings it unusually near the cluster's

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.960
bright stars from our viewpoint. Watch for the pair to separate gradually over the next several

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:54.000
mornings as Mars continues its eastward motion. On a completely different note, a sizeable asteroid

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.880
is set to pass Earth this weekend. The object draws attention from observers around the world.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.380
It is large enough to be monitored by both professional and amateur telescopes.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.840
This flyby will help refine orbital predictions for the asteroid.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:13.300
At the same time, the event allows teams to test their detection networks.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.980
Anyone interested can find details in regular space weather updates.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.980
Viewing guidance is also provided through those same updates,

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:28.720
while we track objects passing Earth, researchers are tracking what our own hardware leaves behind on

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:34.540
the way down. Rocket reentry events leave measurable metal traces in the upper atmosphere.

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:40.880
Researchers have linked specific reentry events to sudden spikes in atmospheric metal concentrations.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:47.600
The data come from ground-based sampling that captured elevated levels of elements commonly used

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:54.080
in spacecraft construction. These measurements show how material from returning stages disperses at

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.600
altitudes where it can interact with the stratosphere. Follow-up studies aim to quantify how frequently

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:07.720
such injections occur as launch rates rise. That atmospheric finding gains extra clarity when we look

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:14.460
at orbit itself from a new perspective. A satellite tracking tool now includes an option that removes

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:21.100
artificial magnification, so objects appear at their real sizes. Users can select the International

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:26.800
Space Station and watch the view zoom out until the station shrinks to a single pixel against the

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:32.540
globe. The update incorporates accurate three-dimensional models for the International Space Station,

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:39.420
Hubble, Tiangong, and thousands of Starlink satellites. Real-time propagation still runs on

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.100
cellist track data, but the visual now matches the physical spacing of objects in orbit.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.620
Even the crowded shells of Starlink satellites become widely separated points against the planet's curve.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.360
The same view shows why collision warnings remain rare despite thousands of objects.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:04.380
One open question is how future mega-constallations will alter that spacing once every orbital shell

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:11.940
fills to higher densities. The loudest gravitational wave signal yet helps probe black hole event horizons.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:18.420
The detection supplies new data on the boundary beyond which nothing escapes a black hole's gravity.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.940
Analysts examine the waveforms fine structure to extract information about space-time geometry

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:31.420
right at the horizon. Continued observations of similar loud events could tighten constraints on how

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:38.200
matter and energy behave in these extreme regions. Extreme physics at the edge of black holes contrast

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:44.280
with the practical engineering of getting humans back to the moon. Lawmakers have introduced legislation

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.280
to honor the four astronauts selected for the first crude flight of the Orion spacecraft.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:55.180
The metal would recognize the mission's role in returning humans to the vicinity of the moon.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:01.040
If approved, it would join a short list of space flight achievements recognized at this level.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:07.320
Policy recognition for the crew pairs naturally with the hardware that will support them on the surface.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:13.360
NASA selected providers to build iterative versions of earlier lunar rover concepts.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.980
The goal is to deliver operational rovers ahead of the Artemis 4 crew arrival,

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.120
so astronauts have mobility from the start of surface operations.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:29.120
The contracts emphasize reduced development time and lower costs compared with previous vehicle

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.680
programs. Surface mobility depends on another key technology now moving from test to flight hardware.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:42.020
Engineers evaluated a prototype nozzle and receiver system designed to transfer propellants

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:47.560
between spacecraft. The hardware must maintain seals at extremely low temperatures while

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:53.480
allowing rapid connection and disconnection. Successful tests move the technology closer to

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:59.880
demonstrations that could support longer deep space missions. Refueling tech helps long duration

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:06.440
missions. Another NASA program is already gathering the atmospheric data those missions will need.

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:12.360
The Balneo project will fly a balloon network to sample stratospheric aerosols.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:18.040
The initiative aims to gather regular, institute measurements of particles that influence

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:23.620
global temperatures and the water cycle. Multiple balloon platforms will operate at different

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:30.340
longitudes to capture variability that satellites see only intermittently. Data will feed into climate

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:36.920
models that currently rely heavily on remote sensing averages. While balloon study our own atmosphere,

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:43.740
Hubble has been examining stellar birth in an ancient cluster. Hubble images an ancient globular

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:50.200
cluster showing two distinct star formation episodes. The cluster contains tens of thousands

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:57.000
to millions of stars packed under mutual gravity. Observations reveal one population of stars that

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:02.660
formed early and a second group that appeared later suggesting the cluster experienced separate

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:09.320
bursts of star birth. More than 150 such clusters are known in the Milky Way, though others may

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:15.600
remain hidden behind dust. Understanding stellar history feeds into student work on keeping future

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:22.700
crews alive. University teams win NASA's 2026 human lander challenge for life support concepts.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:29.460
Student groups presented NVR mental control and life support designs intended for future crude

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:35.660
lunar landers. The winning entries addressed air revitalization, water recovery, and thermal

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:41.920
management under lunar gravity conditions. The competition feeds directly into technology development

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:49.080
supporting the Artemis program. Student ideas for life support connect to a 2024 Geomagnetic storm

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:54.980
that rewrote assumptions about Earth's own atmosphere. Measurements show that the intense event

00:06:54.980 --> 00:07:02.260
drew ions primarily from the planet rather than the solar wind. The finding revises earlier assumptions

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:08.460
about how ring current populations are replenished during major storms. Continued monitoring will

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:15.300
test whether this source dominance holds for future events. Atmospheric surprises above Earth lead to

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:21.500
an upcoming attempt to raise an observatory's orbit. The Pegasus rocket is prepared to launch an orbit

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:27.940
raising spacecraft for the Swift Observatory. The mission targets are no earlier than June 30th

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:34.400
liftoff from Kwajalena toll. The small upper stage vehicle will dock with the aging Neil

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:39.680
Garell's Swift Observatory and gradually increase its altitude over several months.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.320
The effort marks the first attempt to extend an operational astrophysics mission through

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:52.500
in-space propulsion assistance. Extending existing missions sits alongside a new PBS documentary

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:59.660
looking at the next era of lunar exploration. Season 53 Episode 7 of the Long Running Science

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:05.700
series focuses on current lunar exploration architecture. The program covers lander development,

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:10.520
surface operations, and the role of commercial partners in establishing a presence.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:17.160
Interviews highlight engineering challenges that must be solved before crews can stay for extended periods.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:23.280
Media coverage of lunar plans follows a company already building the ground infrastructure for

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:29.920
rapid rocket reuse. Site visits more than six months ago showed substantial progress on infrastructure

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:36.340
needed for full rocket reuse. The company continues work on a vehicle design for rapid turnaround

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:42.500
between flights. Observers are watching for the next visible milestones in testing and assembly.

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:49.300
From one company's rapid reuse ambitions to a quiet but telling update on how empty low earth

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:56.080
orbit really is, a single large stage can inject kilograms of aluminum, titanium, and iron compounds

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:03.100
directly into the mesosphere during its fiery passage. Those atoms then drift downward over weeks

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:09.540
or months, mixing into the aerosol layer that influences both ozone chemistry and the planet's

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:15.020
radiation balance. If every re-entering rocket stage were compressed into a single layer,

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:21.880
the metal it releases would form a film only a few atoms thick, spread across the entire stratosphere.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:28.220
Yet that tiny addition occurs at altitudes where chemistry proceeds slowly and where natural sources

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:34.000
of those same elements are scarce. No one yet knows whether the cumulative effect of rising

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.680
launch rates will eventually rival volcanic or meteoritic contributions on a global scale.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:46.940
Next time, keep an eye on the Pegasus upper stage docking attempt scheduled for no earlier than June 30th.

